---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import DcaLab from "../../components/DcaLab.tsx";
import NextTool from "../../components/NextTool.astro";
import Sources from "../../components/Sources.astro";
import "../../styles/simtool.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="All at Once, or Bit by Bit?"
  description="Lump sum vs dollar-cost averaging, tested across every start month in 150+ years of real US stock returns. See why investing all at once usually wins — and what averaging actually buys you."
>
  <section class="container wl-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interactive Tool</p>
    <h1>All at once, or bit by bit?</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      You come into a windfall. Do you invest it all today, or feed it in slowly to
      "average out" the risk of buying at the top? It feels safer to spread it out —
      but feelings and math don't always agree. We tested both against every single
      start month in over 150 years of real US market returns. The answer is
      surprisingly lopsided, and the <em>why</em> is the interesting part.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-host">
    <DcaLab client:load />
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-explainer">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Why all-at-once usually wins</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Markets rise more than they fall.</strong> Stocks are up in most
          months, so on average, cash sitting on the sidelines waiting to be invested
          is missing returns. The longer you wait to put money in, the more expected
          growth you give up — which is why lump sum beats averaging most of the time.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Averaging isn't about winning — it's about the range.</strong>
          Spreading the buying in narrows the spread of outcomes: you won't do as well
          in the typical case, but you also won't get the worst outcome of going all-in
          right before a crash. It trades a bit of expected return for a smaller chance
          of regret.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The real enemy is not investing at all.</strong> Both strategies beat
          leaving the money in cash over these horizons. The averaging debate is a
          second-order question; the first-order one is getting invested and staying
          invested.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        One honest caveat: this compares investing a lump you <em>already have</em>.
        Investing each paycheck as it arrives is also "dollar-cost averaging," but
        that's just investing as you earn — there's no lump sitting in cash, so the
        trade-off here doesn't apply. Educational only, not advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Sources tool="dollar-cost-averaging" />

  <NextTool current="/tools/dollar-cost-averaging" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wl-intro {
    padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-md);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .wl-explainer {
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
  }
</style>

<style is:global>
  .ss-headline {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.15rem;
    padding: var(--space-sm) var(--space-md);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--color-accent-soft);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-accent);
  }
  .ss-headline-label {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
  }
  .ss-headline-value {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: var(--step-3);
    line-height: 1.1;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .ss-headline-sub {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
  }
  .ss-stats {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-md);
  }
  .ss-stats > div {
    background: var(--color-surface-alt);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: var(--space-sm);
  }
  .ss-stats dt {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--color-muted);
    margin-bottom: 0.2rem;
  }
  .ss-stats dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
</style>
